  "text": "Solution 3 has the most likely correct final answer, 2, though I can't verify it against the paper here.\n\n- **Solution 1 (answer 1)** takes the velocity exponent as 1/2, which is the regularity of u = ψ_y near a logarithmic singularity. But the statement's velocity exponent is the one matching the paper's stream-function-level regularity, which is larger. Its density exponent is also too low.\n- **Solution 2 (answer 0)** gives a negative density exponent, −1/2. Density is transported, and the stated threshold is a positive regularity level, so this is implausible. Its citation of Lin and Xu is also unverified.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 2)** gives s_v = 3/2 and s_ρ = 1/2. My recollection of the Lin–Xu abstract is that the constructed waves lie in H^{3/2−τ} × H^{1/2−τ}, with non-existence in H^{3/2+τ} × H^{1/2+τ} under the no-eigenvalue condition. That matches this solution's threshold pair. Its Frobenius analysis is plausible, though the velocity step is loosely argued: it treats the stream function's regularity as the velocity exponent, which is the convention the recalled abstract appears to use.\n\nMy confidence is moderate, resting on recall of the abstract rather than a checked source.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
